<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Yi of Chu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/206-bce">206 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Xiong Xin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Yi of Chu, born Xiong Xin, was a grandson of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-huai-of-chu">King Huai of Chu</a> whom rebels against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> enthroned in 208 BCE under the same title, King Huai of Chu, as the figurehead of their rising. After the fall of Qin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> gave him the empty title of Emperor Yi, removed him to the south and had him murdered in 206 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xiong Xin descended from the royal house of the Chu state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, but he was not in the main line of succession: four kings followed his grandfather before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Chu in 223 BCE. Under Qin rule he lived as a commoner. When the Dazexiang Uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> broke out in 209 BCE, other risings followed across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with the aim of restoring the six states Qin had conquered.</p><p>Fan Zeng advised the Chu rebel leader Xiang Liang to find a member of the old royal family and put him on the throne to win popular support. Xiang Liang found Xiong Xin working as a shepherd and installed him in the summer of 208 BCE as King Huai II of Chu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At first the king was a puppet, with power in Xiang Liang's hands. After Xiang Liang was killed at the Battle of Dingtao in the winter of 208 BCE, the king took over the armies of the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> and Lü Chen and began to assert himself. He sent Song Yi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and promised the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> to whoever first entered the Qin heartland. When Song Yi refused to advance to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, the Zhao capital besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a>, Xiang Yu killed him on a false charge of treason and seized his command, which the king reluctantly approved.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> first, in the winter of 207 BCE, and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziying">Ziying</a> surrendered to him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> asked the king for the title instead; the king answered that his earlier promise stood, but Xiang Yu, the stronger of the two, ignored him, named himself Hegemon-King of Western Chu and divided the empire into the Eighteen Kingdoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Emperor Yi tried to delay his removal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a> to Chen County by feigning illness, without success. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> secretly ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-bu">Ying Bu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-rui">Wu Rui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gong-ao">Gong Ao</a> to kill him on the way; Ying Bu's men did so near Chen County, and local people buried him on a hill south-west of the county.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-han-contention">Chu–Han Contention</a> that followed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> used the killing as propaganda, holding a three-day memorial service in 205 BCE and accusing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> of regicide. After founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> he had Zhou Bo, Wang Ling and Fan Kuai hold services for the emperor in their marquisates. Emperor Yi sometimes appears as a door god in Chinese and Taoist temples, usually paired with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziying">Ziying</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Yi of Chu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gong Ao had served as Pillar of State to <b>King Huai of Chu</b>, the figurehead of the rebellion against the Qin dynasty, and led an army into the Nanjun region.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-linjiang">Kingdom of Linjiang</a></li><li><blockquote>Xiang Yu dominated the other kings by military force, but he alienated allies, had his nominal sovereign <b>Emperor Yi of Chu</b> killed in 205 BCE and treated defeated enemies brutally.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-chu">Western Chu</a></li></ul></section>
